This process repeats over many generations, driving the evolution of species over time.

Newton's laws are fundamental in game physics. Here's a quick rundown:

**First Law (Inertia):**
```csharp
// An object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion, unless acted upon by an external force.
public class PhysicsObject {
    public Vector2 velocity = Vector2.zero;
    public void Update() {
        // If no force is applied, velocity remains the same
        velocity = velocity;
    }
}
```
**Second Law (Force and Acceleration):**
```csharp
// Force = Mass x Acceleration
public class PhysicsObject {
    public float mass = 1.0f;
    public Vector2 force = Vector2.zero;
    public void Update() {
        // Calculate acceleration based on force and mass
        Vector2 acceleration = force / mass;
        // Update velocity and position
        velocity += acceleration;
    }
}
```
**Third Law (Action and Reaction):**
```csharp
// For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction
public class PhysicsObject {
    public Vector2 force = Vector2.zero;
    public void OnCollisionEnter(Collision collision) {
        // Apply force to both objects involved in collision
        collision.otherObject.force = -force;
    }
}
```
These laws help us create realistic physics in our games.

