We can view the human body as a formal language, where each cell is a variable that can be in one of several states. We can use a set $C$ to denote the set of all cell types.

We can define a function $f: C \to \{ \text{function} \}$, where $f(c) = f(c)$ denotes the function of cell $c$. We can use the following set of functions to describe the types of cells and their functions:

\begin{align*}
f(c) = \begin{cases}
\text{membrane} & \text{if } c \in \{ \text{cell membrane} \} \\
\text{encapsulate} & \text{if } c \in \{ \text{cell boundary} \} \\
\text{compute} & \text{if } c \in \{ \text{cell body} \} \\
\text{communicate} & \text{if } c \in \{ \text{cell surface} \} \\
\text{store} & \text{if } c \in \{ \text{cell interior} \}
\end{align*}

We can use the following notation to describe the types of cells and their functions:

\begin{align*}
\text{cell} = (c, f(c))
\end{align*}

We can use the following theorem to show that there are $n$ types of cells, where $n$ is the size of the set $C$.

Theorem. For any set $C$, there are $|C|$ types of cells.

Proof. We can construct a bijection between the set $C$ and the set of types of cells. Therefore, the number of types of cells is equal to the size of the set $C$.

