We can view the human body as a formal language, where each cell is a string over an alphabet of possible states. The types of cells are the languages over this alphabet.

Formally, let $C$ be the set of all possible cells in the human body. We can define a function $t:C\to T$, where $T$ is the set of types of cells, as follows:

\begin{align*}
t(\text{amino cell}) &= \text{amino}\\
t(\text{cell}) &= \text{cell}\\
t(\text{cell}) &= \text{cell}\\
\end{align*}

We can show that there are $|T|=7$ types of cells, using the following proof:

\begin{align*}
|T|&=|\{\text{amino},\text{cell},\text{cell},\text{cell}\}|\\
&=|\{\text{amino}\}\cup\{\text{cell}\}\cup\{\text{cell}\}\cup\{\text{cell}\}|\\
&=|\{\text{amino}\}|+|\{\text{cell}\}|+|\{\text{cell}\}|+|\{\text{cell}\}|\\
&=1+1+1+1\\
&=\boxed{7}
\end{align*}

Therefore, there are $7$ types of cells in the human body.

