In the realm of geology, chemists and geologists employ a few methods to determine the age of rocks and fossils. One of the most widely used techniques is Radiometric Dating, which involves the measurement of the decay of radioactive isotopes. For instance, \ce{^{\text{14}}C} (carbon-14) is a radioactive isotope of carbon that decays to \ce{^{\text{14}}N} (nitrogen) with a half-life of approximately \ce{5.7 \times 10^3} years. By measuring the ratio of \ce{^{\text{14}}C} to \ce{^{\text{12}}C} (\ce{^{\text{12}}C} is the stable isotope of carbon), scientists can estimate the age of organic materials, such as wood or bone, that contain carbon. This method is known as Radiocarbon Dating.

Another method is Potassium-Argon Dating, which involves the decay of \ce{^{\text{40}}K} (potassium) to \ce{^{\text{40}}Ar} (argon). This technique is used for rocks that contain potassium, such as granite. By analyzing the ratio of \ce{^{\text{40}}K} to \ce{^{\text{40}}Ar}, scientists can determine the age of the rock.

